  "body": {
    "id": "lem:post-mean-law",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\vartheta\\in\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(0)\\), \\(z=m+\\beta+\\eta\\), where \\(\\eta\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2I_N/T_1)\\) and \\(\\eta\\) is independent of \\(Y_0\\). This law remains exact when the post factors are constant.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "N",
      "T0",
      "T1",
      "sigma",
      "beta",
      "Y0",
      "m",
      "z",
      "eta",
      "vartheta",
      "M0"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "ass:gaussian-errors",
      "ass:post-untreated-model",
      "ass:post-effect-model",
      "ass:observed-post"
    ],
    "justification": "The exact post-mean reduction isolates sparse decoding from post-factor variation without imposing a covariance lower bound.",
    "gap": "HeLiShiMiao2026 uses long-post asymptotics in its fixed-\\(N\\) result; this exact Gaussian reduction covers arbitrary \\(T_1\\), including constant post factors.",
    "consumer": "It supplies the post-period likelihood for simultaneous per-unit effect regions in the Jerusalem-conflict and Beijing-pollution analyses.",
    "proof_tex": "By the observation and additive-effect assumptions,\n\\[\nY_1=Y^{\\mathrm{post}}(1)=\\Lambda G+E_1+\\beta\\mathbf 1_{T_1}^{\\top}.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(T_1^{-1}\\mathbf 1_{T_1}\\), together with the definition of \\(a\\), gives\n\\[\nz=\\Lambda a+\\beta+T_1^{-1}E_1\\mathbf 1_{T_1}=m+\\beta+\\eta,\n\\;\n\\eta=T_1^{-1}E_1\\mathbf 1_{T_1}.\n\\]\nThe entries of \\(E_1\\) are mutually independent \\(\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2)\\) variables by the Gaussian-error assumption.  Hence linearity of Gaussian laws yields\n\\[\n\\eta\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2 I_N/T_1).\n\\]\nThe same assumption makes \\(E_1\\) independent of \\(E_0\\).  Since \\(Y_0=\\Lambda F+E_0\\) and the remaining quantities in this expression are deterministic parameters, \\(\\eta\\) is independent of \\(Y_0\\).  None of these steps uses centered post-factor variation: if \\(G=a\\mathbf 1_{T_1}^{\\top}\\), the same calculation applies verbatim.  Finally, the additive potential-outcome condition gives, for every labeled unit \\(i\\),\n\\[\nT_1^{-1}\\sum_{u=1}^{T_1}\\{Y_{iu}(1)-Y_{iu}(0)\\}=\\beta_i.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\beta\\) is the causal attributable-effect vector, whereas \\(z=m+\\beta+\\eta\\) is the observed-law equation through which it is inferred.",
